\title{Effective Reconstruction: Fibre Constancy, Uniform Representative
Selection, and a Complete Quantity for Effective Decoding}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{12 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $\obs : S \to M$ be a \emph{channel} mapping hidden states to observable records,
and let $f : S \to T$ be a \emph{quantity} an observer wishes to reconstruct from
records alone. Set-theoretically, a decoder $\dec : M \to T$ with
$\dec \circ \obs = f$ exists if and only if $f$ is \emph{fibre constant} along $\obs$.
We study the effective analogue of this equivalence for computable channels and
quantities on $\N$, and show that it fails in a precisely locatable way. We isolate the
missing ingredient: a \emph{uniform effective representative selection on the range}
(``selector''), a computable $\sel$ with $\obs(\sel(\obs(x))) = \obs(x)$ for all $x$.
We prove that fibre constancy always yields a partial computable decoder and a
limit-computable total decoder with the explicit modulus $s > x$, so decoders are always
$\Delta^0_2$; that a computable selector yields total computable decoders for all
computable fibre-constant quantities; that the separation is strict, witnessed by an
explicit diagonal trace channel; that a computable selector exists iff the range of the
channel is computable, while the range is always $\Sigma^0_1$; and that finite-rate
channels are always effectively decodable. The principal new result is a completeness
theorem: the canonical representative $r_{\obs}(n) = \mu m.[\obs(m) = \obs(n)]$ is
computable, fibre constant, and has the property that every decoder for it is a
selector, yielding a four-way equivalence between decidability of the range, existence
of a computable selector, universal effective decodability, and effective decodability
of the single quantity $r_{\obs}$. This removes an injectivity hypothesis from the
previously known partial converse and exhibits a canonical hard instance: the least
halting trace of a self-halting index is not computable from the index. Finally we prove
an effective Fano bound: via a finite-patching principle, on an undecodable channel every
computable decoder errs on infinitely many records and misreconstructs infinitely many
states.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} effective reconstruction; fibre constancy; uniform
selection; decidable range; limit computability; diagonalisation; Fano bound; canonical
representative.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The reconstruction problem}

A recurring pattern across information theory, privacy analysis, dynamical systems and
inverse problems is the following. There is a set $S$ of \emph{states}, invisible to an
observer, a set $M$ of \emph{records}, visible, and a \textbf{channel}
$\obs : S \to M$ determining what the observer sees. The observer is interested not in
the state but in a derived \textbf{quantity} $f : S \to T$. A \textbf{decoder} is a map
$\dec : M \to T$ with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:decode}
  \dec(\obs(x)) = f(x) \qquad \text{for all } x \in S.
\end{equation}
Much of the quantitative theory takes a decoder as \emph{given} and bounds its accuracy,
in rate--distortion or Fano style, over finite state spaces and finite alphabets. The
prior question is one of \textbf{definability}: when does a decoder exist at all, and
when does it exist computably? This paper answers both, and shows that the two answers
differ.

\subsection{The classical answer and its non-effective step}

Say $f$ is \textbf{fibre constant} along $\obs$ if
$\obs(x) = \obs(y) \Rightarrow f(x) = f(y)$. A decoder exists iff $f$ is fibre constant
(Theorem~\ref{thm:def}); the construction reads \emph{given a record $m$, choose a state
$x$ with $\obs(x) = m$ and output $f(x)$}. It is non-effective in exactly one place: the
choice. An observer who must compute cannot choose; it must search, and search on an
infinite state space need not terminate --- and when it does not, the observer cannot
know. Our thesis is that the entire effective content of reconstruction is concentrated
in this step, and that the step has an exact characterisation.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Isolation of the obstruction} (\S\ref{sec:eff}): uniform effective
  representative selection suffices for effective decoding of all computable
  fibre-constant quantities; computably bounded preimage search suffices for its
  existence.
\item \textbf{A strict separation} (\S\ref{sec:diag}): an explicit diagonal trace channel
  carries a computable fibre-constant quantity with no total computable decoder, hence no
  computable selector and no computable preimage bound.
\item \textbf{Exact characterisation of selection} (\S\ref{sec:range}): a computable
  selector exists iff the range is computable; the range is always $\Sigma^0_1$; finite
  rate implies effective decodability.
\item \textbf{A one-jump upper bound} (\S\ref{sec:jump}): decoders are always
  limit-computable with an explicit modulus, and this is sharp.
\item \textbf{Completeness} (\S\ref{sec:complete}): the canonical representative is a
  computable fibre-constant quantity whose decoders are exactly the selectors, yielding a
  four-way equivalence.
\item \textbf{An effective Fano bound} (\S\ref{sec:fano}): every computable decoder on an
  undecodable channel errs infinitely often.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\varphi_a$ denotes the $a$-th partial computable function under a fixed
standard numbering and $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ a computable pairing bijection
$\N^2 \to \N$. ``Computable set'' means decidable; $\Sigma^0_1$ means recursively
enumerable; $\Delta^0_2$ means limit-computable.

\begin{definition}[Fibre constancy]
For $\obs : \alpha \to \beta$ and $f : \alpha \to \gamma$, $f$ is \emph{fibre constant
along} $\obs$ if $\obs(x) = \obs(y)$ implies $f(x) = f(y)$; equivalently $f$ is constant
on each fibre $\obs^{-1}\{m\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Decoding]
$\dec : \beta \to \gamma$ \emph{decodes} $f$ through $\obs$ if $\dec(\obs(x)) = f(x)$ for
every $x$. Condition \eqref{eq:decode} constrains $\dec$ only on $\ran(\obs)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform representative selection on the range]\label{def:sel}
$\sel : \beta \to \alpha$ is a \emph{selector} for $\obs$ if
$\obs(\sel(\obs(x))) = \obs(x)$ for all $x$: it maps each record in the range to some
state in the corresponding fibre. Off the range it is unconstrained.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Effective decodability]
A computable channel $\obs : \N \to \N$ is \emph{effectively decodable} if every
computable fibre-constant $f : \N \to \N$ admits a total computable decoder.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical representative]\label{def:rep}
For $\obs : \N \to \N$ set
$r_{\obs}(n) = \min\{ m \in \N : \obs(m) = \obs(n)\}$. The set contains $n$, so
$r_{\obs}$ is total.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rate]
The \emph{rate} of a channel is the cardinality of its range; the channel has
\emph{finite rate} if the range is finite.
\end{definition}

\section{The set-theoretic theory}

\begin{theorem}[Definability of decoders]\label{thm:def}
Let $\alpha$ be nonempty, $\obs : \alpha \to \beta$, $f : \alpha \to \gamma$. A decoder
for $f$ through $\obs$ exists iff $f$ is fibre constant along $\obs$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $\dec$ decodes $f$ and $\obs(x)=\obs(y)$ then
$f(x)=\dec(\obs(x))=\dec(\obs(y))=f(y)$. ($\Leftarrow$) Set $\dec(m)=f(x_m)$ for a chosen
$x_m \in \obs^{-1}\{m\}$ when this fibre is nonempty and an arbitrary value otherwise. The
fibre of $\obs(x)$ is nonempty and fibre constancy gives $f(x_{\obs(x)}) = f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Selection composes to decoding]\label{prop:comp}
If $f$ is fibre constant along $\obs$ and $\sel$ is a selector for $\obs$, then
$f \circ \sel$ decodes $f$ through $\obs$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\obs(\sel(\obs(x))) = \obs(x)$, and $f$ is constant on that fibre, so
$f(\sel(\obs(x))) = f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Finite baseline]\label{prop:fin}
If $\alpha$ is finite and nonempty and $\beta$ has decidable equality, every
$\obs : \alpha \to \beta$ admits a selector.
\end{proposition}

Proposition~\ref{prop:fin} explains why the phenomena below are invisible to the finite
theory: on a finite state space definability is never an obstruction and accuracy is the
only issue.

\section{The effective theory}\label{sec:eff}

From here $\obs, f : \N \to \N$ are computable.

\begin{theorem}[Partial computable decoders always exist]\label{thm:partial}
If $\obs, f$ are computable and $f$ is fibre constant along $\obs$, there is a partial
computable $\dec$ with $f(x) \in \dec(\obs(x))$ for all $x$; that is, $\dec$ is defined
and correct on the whole range of $\obs$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $\dec(m) = f\big(\mu n.[\obs(n)=m]\big)$. The predicate $\obs(n)=m$ is computable in
$(m,n)$, so $\dec$ is partial computable. If $m = \obs(x)$ the search succeeds, since $x$
itself is a witness, returning some $n$ with $\obs(n) = \obs(x)$; fibre constancy gives
$f(n) = f(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The effective obstruction is never \emph{computation}; it is \emph{totalisation}. The
algorithm is correct wherever it converges.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Effective selection gives effective decoding]\label{thm:seldec}
If $\obs$ admits a computable selector $\sel$, then every computable fibre-constant $f$
has the total computable decoder $f \circ \sel$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:comp}; composites of computable functions are computable.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Computably bounded search suffices]\label{thm:bounded}
Let $\obs$ be computable and suppose there is a computable $b$ with
$\forall x\,\exists y \le b(\obs(x)),\ \obs(y) = \obs(x)$. Then $\obs$ admits a computable
selector.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\sel(t) = \mu n.[\obs(n) = t \ \vee\ b(t) \le n]$. The guard is computable and holds
at $n = b(t)$ at the latest, so $\sel$ is total computable. Let $t = \obs(x)$ and suppose
the returned $n$ has $\obs(n) \neq t$; then $b(t) \le n$. The hypothesis gives a preimage
$y \le b(t) \le n$, and $y \ne n$, so $y < n$; minimality of $n$ makes the guard false at
$y$, contradicting $\obs(y)=t$.
\end{proof}

\section{The diagonal trace channel}\label{sec:diag}

\begin{construction}[Diagonal trace channel]\label{con:diag}
Let $\mathrm{run}(n)$ be the step-bounded evaluation which, on $n = \langle a,s\rangle$,
runs the $a$-th machine on input $a$ for $s$ steps, yielding $\mathsf{some}\ v$ if it
halts with output $v$ and $\mathsf{none}$ otherwise; this is computable. Define
\[
\obsD(\langle a,s\rangle) =
\begin{cases} a+1, & \mathrm{run} = \mathsf{some}\ v,\\ 0, & \text{otherwise,}\end{cases}
\qquad
\fD(\langle a,s\rangle) =
\begin{cases} v+1, & \mathrm{run} = \mathsf{some}\ v,\\ 0, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
Both are computable. The record reveals \emph{which} machine self-halted, not \emph{what}
it computed.
\end{construction}

\begin{lemma}[Fibre constancy of the halting value]\label{lem:fcdiag}
$\fD$ is fibre constant along $\obsD$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If both traces are non-halting, both records and both values are $0$. A halting and a
non-halting trace have different records, since $a+1 \neq 0$. If both halt with equal
records then $a_x = a_y$, so the two traces run the same machine on the same input with
different step bounds; step-bounded evaluation is sound for the underlying partial
function, which takes at most one value at a point, so the outputs agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation]\label{thm:sep}
There is no total computable $\dec$ with $\dec(\obsD(n)) = \fD(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose there is, and put $g(a) = \dec(a+1)$, a total computable function. Let $a_0$ be an
index for $g$. Since $g$ is total it halts on $a_0$ with value $g(a_0)$, so by
completeness of step-bounded evaluation there is $s$ with
$\mathrm{run}(\langle a_0,s\rangle) = \mathsf{some}\ g(a_0)$. For $n = \langle a_0,s\rangle$
we get $\obsD(n) = a_0+1$ and $\fD(n) = g(a_0)+1$, so correctness yields
$g(a_0) = \dec(a_0+1) = \fD(n) = g(a_0)+1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No computable selector]\label{cor:nosel}
$\obsD$ admits no computable selector.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:seldec} would produce the decoder excluded by Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No computable preimage bound]
There is no computable $b$ with
$\forall x\,\exists y \le b(\obsD(x)),\ \obsD(y) = \obsD(x)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded} would produce the selector excluded by
Corollary~\ref{cor:nosel}.
\end{proof}

\section{Selection is decidability of the range}\label{sec:range}

\begin{theorem}[A selector certifies the range]\label{thm:cert}
If $\obs$ is computable and admits a computable selector $\sel$, then $\ran(\obs)$ is a
computable set.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $m = \obs(n)$ then $\obs(\sel(m)) = m$ by Definition~\ref{def:sel}; conversely
$\obs(\sel(m)) = m$ exhibits $\sel(m)$ as a preimage. So membership in the range is the
computable condition $\obs(\sel(m)) = m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A decidable range produces a selector]\label{thm:guard}
If $\obs$ is computable with computable range then $\obs$ admits a computable selector.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Guarded search: $\sel(t) = \mu n.[\obs(n) = t \ \vee\ t \notin \ran(\obs)]$. The guard is
computable by hypothesis, so the search is total: on records in the range it finds a
preimage, off the range it halts immediately. On records in the range the first disjunct
is the one satisfied.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation]\label{thm:char}
For computable $\obs$, a computable selector exists iff $\ran(\obs)$ is a computable set.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The range is always enumerable]\label{thm:re}
For computable $\obs$, $\ran(\obs)$ is $\Sigma^0_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The partial computable map $m \mapsto \mu n.[\obs(n)=m]$ has domain exactly $\ran(\obs)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The emitted-record set of a computable channel is always recursively enumerable, and is
computable precisely when the channel admits a computable selector. Undecidability of the
range is therefore the only possible obstruction to effective reconstruction.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Undecidable range of the diagonal trace channel]
$\ran(\obsD)$ is not computable; equivalently
$\{0\} \cup \{a+1 : \varphi_a(a)\!\downarrow\}$ is undecidable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Finite rate implies effective decodability]
If $\obs$ is computable with finite range, every computable fibre-constant $f$ has a total
computable decoder.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A finite set of naturals is computable, so Theorem~\ref{thm:guard} gives a selector and
Theorem~\ref{thm:seldec} the decoder.
\end{proof}

\section{The gap is exactly one jump}\label{sec:jump}

\begin{definition}[Stage-$s$ approximate decoder]
For computable $\obs, f$ define $A(m,0) = 0$ and
$A(m,k+1) = f(k)$ if $\obs(k) = m$, else $A(m,k)$. Thus $A(m,s)$ scans the states
$0,\dots,s-1$ and keeps the $f$-value of the last one whose record is $m$. $A$ is
computable in $(m,s)$ by primitive recursion.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence with an explicit modulus]\label{thm:mod}
If $f$ is fibre constant along $\obs$ then $A(\obs(x), s) = f(x)$ for every $x$ and every
$s > x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $s$. At $s = x+1$ the last scanned state is $x$ itself, whose record matches,
so $A = f(x)$. For $s = k+1 > x+1$: if $\obs(k) = \obs(x)$ the new value is
$f(k) = f(x)$ by fibre constancy; otherwise it is inherited from stage $k$, which equals
$f(x)$ by induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Limit-computable decoders always exist]
Every computable fibre-constant quantity admits a computable double sequence converging to
the decoded value with modulus ``any preimage of the received record''. In particular the
decoder is $\Delta^0_2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness]
For $\obsD, \fD$ the decoder is limit-computable with modulus $s > x$ but not computable.
Uniform effective representative selection is therefore strictly stronger than fibre
constancy and strictly weaker than any obstruction beyond one limit.
\end{corollary}

\section{A complete quantity and the four-way equivalence}\label{sec:complete}

Theorems~\ref{thm:seldec} and~\ref{thm:char} leave one gap: does universal effective
decodability imply a selector? For \emph{injective} channels this is immediate --- take
$f = \mathrm{id}$, vacuously fibre constant, and any decoder for it is a selector --- but
injectivity trivialises fibre constancy. The general case requires a single quantity hard
for the whole decoding problem. Recall $r_{\obs}$ from Definition~\ref{def:rep}.

\begin{lemma}[The representative lies in the fibre]\label{lem:repspec}
$\obs(r_{\obs}(n)) = \obs(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Fibre constancy]\label{lem:repfc}
$r_{\obs}$ is fibre constant along $\obs$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $\obs(x) = \obs(y)$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:repspec} $\obs(r_{\obs}(y)) = \obs(x)$, so
$r_{\obs}(y)$ competes in the minimisation defining $r_{\obs}(x)$, giving
$r_{\obs}(x) \le r_{\obs}(y)$; symmetrically for the reverse inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Computability of the canonical representative]\label{thm:repcomp}
If $\obs$ is computable then so is $r_{\obs}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$r_{\obs}(n) = \mu m.[\obs(m) = \obs(n)]$, a minimisation of a predicate computable in
$(m,n)$, hence partial computable; and it is total, because $m = n$ satisfies the
predicate, so the search terminates by $n$ at the latest.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The unbounded search of Theorem~\ref{thm:partial} takes a \emph{record} as input; the
search here takes a \emph{state}, which is its own witness. This asymmetry is why
$r_{\obs}$ is always a legitimate computable quantity even though its decoder need not
exist.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Every decoder for the canonical representative is a selector]\label{thm:repsel}
If $\dec(\obs(x)) = r_{\obs}(x)$ for all $x$, then $\dec$ is a selector for $\obs$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\obs(\dec(\obs(x))) = \obs(r_{\obs}(x)) = \obs(x)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:repspec}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Four-way equivalence]\label{thm:fourway}
Let $\obs : \N \to \N$ be computable. The following are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The set of emitted records $\{m : \exists n,\ \obs(n) = m\}$ is computable.
\item $\obs$ admits a computable uniform representative selection on its range.
\item Every computable fibre-constant $f : \N \to \N$ admits a total computable decoder
  through $\obs$.
\item The single quantity $r_{\obs}$ admits a total computable decoder through $\obs$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$1 \Rightarrow 2$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:guard}; $2 \Rightarrow 3$ is
Theorem~\ref{thm:seldec}; $3 \Rightarrow 4$ applies (3) to $r_{\obs}$, legitimate by
Theorem~\ref{thm:repcomp} and Lemma~\ref{lem:repfc}; $4 \Rightarrow 2$ is
Theorem~\ref{thm:repsel}; $2 \Rightarrow 1$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Canonical hard instance]
No algorithm, given the index of a machine known to halt on itself, can produce a trace
witnessing that halting: there is no total computable $\dec$ with
$\dec(\obsD(n)) = r_{\obsD}(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Such a decoder would be a selector by Theorem~\ref{thm:repsel}, contradicting
Corollary~\ref{cor:nosel}.
\end{proof}

\section{An effective Fano bound}\label{sec:fano}

\begin{lemma}[Single-point patching]\label{lem:patch1}
If $\dec$ is computable then so is
$y \mapsto \mathbf{if}\ y = y_0\ \mathbf{then}\ c\ \mathbf{else}\ \dec(y)$ for any
constants $y_0, c$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Finite patching principle]\label{lem:patch}
Let $g : \N \to \N$ be arbitrary (possibly uncomputable), $L$ a finite list and $\dec$
computable. Then $y \mapsto \mathbf{if}\ y \in L\ \mathbf{then}\ g(y)\ \mathbf{else}\ \dec(y)$
is computable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $L$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:patch1}; only the finitely many constants $g(y)$,
$y \in L$, occur, and each can be hard-wired.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Repair lemma]\label{thm:repair}
Let $\obs, f$ be computable with $f$ fibre constant and let $\dec$ be computable and wrong
only on records from a finite list $L$, i.e.\ $\dec(\obs(n)) \neq f(n) \Rightarrow \obs(n) \in L$.
Then a perfect computable decoder for $f$ through $\obs$ exists.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $g(m)$ be the correct decoded value on records in the range --- well defined by fibre
constancy --- and arbitrary elsewhere, and patch $\dec$ by $g$ on $L$. The result is
computable by Lemma~\ref{lem:patch} and correct on every state: by the patch when the
record lies in $L$, and by hypothesis otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Effective Fano bound]\label{thm:fano}
Let $\obs, f$ be computable with $f$ fibre constant and with no total computable decoder.
Then for every computable $\dec$ the set of misdecoded records
$\{m : \exists n,\ \obs(n)=m \wedge \dec(m) \neq f(n)\}$ is infinite, and so is the set of
misreconstructed states $\{n : \dec(\obs(n)) \neq f(n)\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If the record error set were finite, Theorem~\ref{thm:repair} with $L$ that finite set
would produce a perfect computable decoder. The state error set maps onto the record error
set under $\obs$, and images of finite sets are finite, so it is infinite too.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every computable $\dec$ misreconstructs infinitely many states of the diagonal trace
channel, and errs on infinitely many distinct records; likewise every computable attempt
to compute $r_{\obsD}$ from the record errs on infinitely many states.
\end{corollary}

In the finite theory a Fano-type bound gives a lower bound of (number of states) minus
(rate) on the number of misreconstructed configurations. Theorem~\ref{thm:fano} is its
effective counterpart, with lower bound $\infty$, uniform over all algorithms.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Guarded selector from a decidable range]
Input a record $t$. If $t \notin \ran(\obs)$ (decidable by hypothesis) return $0$;
otherwise return the least $n$ with $\obs(n) = t$. The guard makes the procedure total.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Bounded selector]
Given a computable budget $b$, return the least $n \le b(t)$ with $\obs(n) = t$, else
$b(t)$. Cost $O(b(t))$ channel evaluations; correct on the range when $b$ bounds a
preimage.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Canonical representative]
Input a state $n$; return the least $m$ with $\obs(m) = \obs(n)$. Terminates within $n+1$
channel evaluations since $m=n$ is a witness --- a total computable function even when the
range is undecidable.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Stage-$s$ approximate decoder]
Input a record $m$ and budget $s$; scan $k = 0,\dots,s-1$ keeping $f(k)$ for the last $k$
with $\obs(k) = m$, returning $0$ if none. Cost $O(s)$; correct at $(\obs(x),s)$ whenever
$s > x$, though stabilisation is not in general recognisable.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Repair]
Given a computable decoder and a finite list of bad records with correct values, return the
patched decoder; cost $O(|L|)$ comparisons plus one call to the original decoder.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Three notions, three characterisations}

