\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Information present versus information available}

Fibre constancy is an information-theoretic condition: the channel does not conflate states
with different answers. The selector condition is an effective one: the observer can find
its way back into a fibre. The diagonal trace channel separates them dramatically.
Determinism guarantees the record \emph{determines} the halting value; diagonalisation
guarantees the observer cannot compute it. No information is lost and none is available.
This bears on informal arguments about privacy and reconstruction: ``cannot be recovered''
and ``can in principle be recovered'' are separated here by a single decidable-or-not
invariant, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:fourway} the invariant is all-or-nothing.

\subsection{Limits of the analysis}

The results are stated for $\N$-valued channels; extensions to other computable domains are
routine via effective codings. The $\Delta^0_2$ bound concerns the decoder, not uniformity
in the quantity: computing a decoder index from a quantity index would require $\emptyset'$
and is a separate matter. Nothing here addresses resource-bounded reconstruction; the
bounded criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded} is the natural entry point there.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree-theoretic calibration]
For every computably enumerable Turing degree $\mathbf{d}$ there is a computable channel
whose decoding problem --- equivalently whose canonical representative $r_{\obs}$ --- has
Turing degree exactly $\mathbf{d}$, and the map from channels to degrees is onto the c.e.
degrees.
\end{conjecture}

The range of a computable channel is always $\Sigma^0_1$ and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:char},
effective decoding is exactly $\Delta^0_1$-ness of that range; the decoding problem
therefore inherits the whole structure of the c.e.\ degrees rather than a single halting
phenomenon. Theorem~\ref{thm:fourway} reduces the conjecture to a statement about ranges of
computable functions, where Post-style constructions are standard.

\begin{conjecture}[Modulus dichotomy]
For a computable channel the decoder is computable iff the convergence modulus of the
stage-$s$ approximation is dominated by a computable function; and there exist channels
whose modulus is dominated by no computable function yet whose error density --- the
fraction of states below $N$ decoded incorrectly by the best budget-$N$ decoder --- tends
to $0$.
\end{conjecture}

The approximation of \S\ref{sec:jump} has the canonical modulus ``any preimage'', so the
obstruction is concentrated in the growth rate of the least-preimage function. Such a
channel would be worst-case undecodable and average-case benign.

Two further directions: replacing ``computable'' by ``polynomial time'' throughout, turning
Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded} into a statement about polynomially bounded preimage search; and
weakening Definition~\ref{def:sel} to selection on a dense subset of the range, where the
effective Fano bound suggests density of failure is the right measure.

\section{Conclusion}

Reconstruction splits into definability and accuracy, and definability splits further into
what the record determines and what the observer can compute. Fibre constancy settles the
first and yields partial and limit-computable decoders; uniform effective representative
selection settles the second and equals decidability of the emitted-record set. The
canonical representative --- the least state producing the same record --- is computable,
fibre constant and complete for the problem, collapsing the theory into a four-way
equivalence and pinning the obstruction to an explicit hard instance: finding a halting
trace from a halting index. Where the obstruction bites it bites everywhere: every
algorithm errs infinitely often. Where it does not, and in particular at finite rate, one
uniform recipe decodes every computable fibre-constant quantity at once.

